  "lineage": "UTO — complete issued Plumbing (2024),2:55, from When all you want to do is be the fire part of fire. In the firsthand production interview Neysa and Émile describe the whole minimal track: autonomous Gene III drum-machine pattern, piano, Neysa singing while ill with retained nose/tissue noises, and Mellotron added at the end. Its master processing and compression are positively acknowledged; no specific processor or exact routing is asserted. Other tracks’ guests, drums, Osmose and James Brown sample are not transferred. Two creators does not imply two singers on this track; Neysa is the positively identified singer, unspecified extra vocal layering is not counted as another person. This is the complete issued track described by its makers, not an unheard whole album inferred from one passage. Source-specific Gene III grid-free autonomous timing is retained in a complete isolated control surface, with unknown pitches, decays, levels, assignments and routing explicitly neutral. No optional expansion module is asserted. Existing Mellotron M400 polyester-tape strings is an authored coherent realization because source says Mellotron without model or tape voice; this is not a claim to source strings. Modern studio upright and chordal keyboard gesture are authored realizations of source-unspecified piano. One accompanied voice with unspecified physiology and pitch system; single lead take is authored rather than measured layering. Retained tissue/noise role uses existing mechanism-unspecified sound-effect owner, not a fabricated sampler. Spacious hall-like reverberant listening presentation is authored, not a live-concert or source-site assertion; global tuning/capture chain omitted. Source-positive master processing is acknowledged but specific transfer function remains unknown. All five source sound roles retained. The positive compressed master is represented by an explicitly authored transparent multiband vocal-master option; multiband architecture is not claimed as the original processor. This avoids claiming the positively treated voice was unprocessed.",
